[38;2;124;124;124mhas cardinality of the continuum, without assuming CH or its negation?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, the answer: No, we cannot conclude that. Let me think about why.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA set B ⊆ ℝ that is not Borel-measurable. The question is whether |B| must equal[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe continuum c = |ℝ|.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey facts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Borel sets: there are only c many Borel sets (the Borel σ-algebra has [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcardinality c).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Every Borel set is either countable or has cardinality c (this is a theorem: [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mevery uncountable Borel set has cardinality c, because every uncountable Borel [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mset contains a perfect subset, and perfect sets have cardinality c).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo Borel sets are "small" in terms of cardinality dichotomy: countable or c.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, B is NOT Borel. What can |B| be?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question: must |B| = c?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mConsider: B ⊆ ℝ, B not Borel. Could B be countable? No — every countable subset [0m[38;2;124;124;124mof ℝ is Borel (countable sets are F_σ, hence Borel). So B must be uncountable.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mCould |B| be strictly between ℵ₀ and c? That's the CH question. If ¬CH, there [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexist subsets of ℝ with cardinality strictly between ℵ₀ and c. But are such sets[0m[38;2;124;124;124mnecessarily non-Borel? Yes! Because every Borel set is either countable or has [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcardinality c. So any set of cardinality strictly between ℵ₀ and c is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mautomatically not Borel.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo under ¬CH, there exist sets of intermediate cardinality, and they are all [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnon-Borel. So under ¬CH, there's a non-Borel set of cardinality < c.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut the question is: without assuming CH or ¬CH, can we conclude |B| = c?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe answer is NO. Because:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Under ¬CH (which is consistent with ZFC, by Cohen's work), there exist subsets[0m[38;2;124;124;124mof ℝ with cardinality strictly between ℵ₀ and c. Such a set is uncountable but [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mhas cardinality < c, hence is not Borel (since Borel sets are countable or c). [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSo under ¬CH, there exists a non-Borel set B with |B| < c.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, but the question is whether we can *conclude* it in ZFC alone. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexistence of such a set requires ¬CH. In ZFC + CH, every uncountable subset... [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mno wait, that's not right either. Under CH, every uncountable subset of ℝ has [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcardinality c = ℵ₁. So under CH, every uncountable subset of ℝ has cardinality [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mc, and since non-Borel sets must be uncountable, they all have cardinality c.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Under CH: every non-Borel set has cardinality c. ✓[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Under ¬CH: there exist non-Borel sets of cardinality strictly between ℵ₀ and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mc. ✗[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince both CH and ¬CH are consistent with ZFC (assuming ZFC is consistent), ZFC [0m[38;2;124;124;124malone cannot prove that every non-Borel set has cardinality c.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut wait — the question asks "can one conclude that B necessarily has the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcardinality of the continuum." This is asking whether ZFC proves this statement.[0m[38;2;124;124;124mThe answer is no, because ¬CH is consistent with ZFC, and under ¬CH there are [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcounterexamples.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut actually, let me be more careful. The question is about a specific B that is[0m[38;2;124;124;124mnot Borel. It asks: can we conclude |B| = c? This is asking whether ZFC ⊢ "every[0m[38;2;124;124;124mnon-Borel subset of ℝ has cardinality c."[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTo show the answer is NO, we need to show that it's consistent with ZFC that [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthere exists a non-Borel subset of ℝ with cardinality ≠ c.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mUnder ¬CH: Let κ be a cardinal with ℵ₀ < κ < c. There exists a subset A ⊆ ℝ with[0m[38;2;124;124;124m|A| = κ. Since A is uncountable and |A| < c, and every Borel set is either [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcountable or has cardinality c, A cannot be Borel. So A is a non-Borel set with [0m[38;2;124;124;124m|A| = κ < c.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo under ¬CH, the statement "every non-Borel set has cardinality c" is false.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince ¬CH is consistent with ZFC (Cohen 1963), ZFC cannot prove "every non-Borel[0m[38;2;124;124;124mset has cardinality c."[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore, the answer is: No, one cannot conclude that B necessarily has the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcardinality of the continuum.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me also verify the key lemma: every uncountable Borel set has cardinality c.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mTheorem (Perfect Set Theorem for Borel sets): Every uncountable Borel subset of [0m[38;2;124;124;124mℝ contains a perfect subset. Since perfect subsets of ℝ have cardinality c, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mevery uncountable Borel set has cardinality c.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a classical result, provable in ZFC. The proof uses the fact that Borel [0m[38;2;124;124;124msets can be built up from open sets via countable unions and complements, and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mone shows by transfinite induction that every Borel set has the perfect set [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mproperty.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, more precisely: every analytic set (Σ¹₁) has the perfect set property,[0m[38;2;124;124;124mand Borel sets are analytic. This is Souslin's theorem area... actually the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mperfect set property for Borel sets is easier and was known before (Suslin's [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtheorem is that Borel = Δ¹₁, and analytic sets have the perfect set property is [0m[38;2;124;124;124ma result of Suslin/Alexandrov).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the perfect set property for Borel sets: This is provable in ZFC. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mEvery uncountable Borel set contains a perfect subset. Perfect sets have [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcardinality c. So uncountable Borel sets have cardinality c.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write up the proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mProof structure:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. Lemma: Every Borel subset of ℝ is either countable or has cardinality c. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(Perfect set property for Borel sets)[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. Corollary: Every non-Borel subset of ℝ is uncountable (since countable sets [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mare Borel).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3. Under ¬CH (consistent with ZFC): there exists κ with ℵ₀ < κ < c, and a subset[0m[38;2;124;124;124mA ⊆ ℝ with |A| = κ. By Lemma, A is not Borel, but |A| < c.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m4. Therefore ZFC cannot prove "every non-Borel set has cardinality c."[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m5. Answer: No.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, I should also address: is the statement independent of ZFC? Under CH, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe statement is true (vacuously, since every uncountable subset has cardinality[0m[38;2;124;124;124mc). Under ¬CH, it's false. So the statement is independent of ZFC (equivalent to[0m[38;2;124;124;124mCH, actually).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me verify: Under CH, c = ℵ₁. Every uncountable subset of ℝ has cardinality ≥[0m[38;2;124;124;124mℵ₁ = c, so cardinality = c. So every non-Borel set (which is uncountable) has [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcardinality c. True.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mUnder ¬CH, c > ℵ₁. There's a subset of cardinality ℵ₁ < c, which is uncountable [0m[38;2;124;124;124mbut not c, hence not Borel. False.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the statement "every non-Borel subset of ℝ has cardinality c" is equivalent [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mto CH over ZFC.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me be careful about the equivalence. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124m- CH ⟹ "every non-Borel set has cardinality c": Yes, as shown.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- "every non-Borel set has cardinality c" ⟹ CH: Suppose ¬CH. Then there's a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msubset A with ℵ₀ < |A| < c. A is not Borel (by perfect set property). |A| < c. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mContradiction. So the statement implies ¬¬CH, i.e., CH.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo yes, the statement is equivalent to CH.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore the answer is: No, one cannot conclude this in ZFC alone. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstatement is equivalent to CH and is therefore independent of ZFC.[0m

